      "Normal Physiology": "von Willebrand factor (vWF) is a large multimeric plasma glycoprotein synthesized by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes. It performs two vital hemostatic functions: (1) it acts as an essential bridging adhesive molecule that binds platelet membrane receptors (GPIb) to exposed subendothelial collagen at sites of vascular injury, mediating primary platelet adhesion, and (2) it serves as the stabilizing carrier protein for Factor VIII in the circulation, protecting Factor VIII from rapid enzymatic degradation.",
