      "Name with Synonyms": "Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC), Consumption Coagulopathy, Defibrination Syndrome.",
      "Definition": "A complex clinicopathological syndrome characterized by widespread, systemic activation of the blood coagulation system, leading to extensive microvascular thrombosis, consumption of platelets and clotting factors, and secondary hyperfibrinolysis resulting in severe bleeding.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Blood fluidity is normally preserved by a strict balance between procoagulants, endothelial inhibitors (thrombomodulin, prostacyclin, EDRF), circulating natural anticoagulants (Antithrombin III, Protein C, Protein S), and localized fibrinolysis.",
      "Etiology": "Triggered by massive introduction or exposure of procoagulant stimuli—primarily tissue factor (tissue thromboplastin)—into the systemic circulation. Major causes include:\n- Obstetric Emergencies: Septic abortions, amniotic fluid embolism, pre-eclampsia/eclampsia.\n- Infections: Gram-negative bacterial septicemia (release of bacterial endotoxins), severe sepsis, fungemia.\n- Tissue Trauma: Severe crush injury, extensive burns, major surgery.\n- Others: Snake bites, acute hemolytic transfusion reactions.",
      "Site": "Microvasculature (small peripheral blood vessels, capillaries, arterioles) across multiple organ systems.",
      "Pathophysiology": "1. Microvascular Thrombosis: Uncontrolled tissue factor exposure directly activates Factor VII, triggering widespread extrinsic and intrinsic coagulation cascades.\n2. Nutritional & Hypoxic Failure: Widespread microclots plug small peripheral vessels, diminishing oxygen/nutrient delivery and precipitating tissue ischemia and multiorgan failure.\n3. Consumption Coagulopathy: Massive clot formation depletes circulating platelets, fibrinogen, Factor V, and Factor VIII beyond the liver's synthetic capacity.\n4. Secondary Fibrinolysis: Widespread fibrin deposition triggers extensive activation of plasminogen to plasmin, which degrades fibrin into massive quantities of Fibrin Degradation Products (FDPs) that further inhibit platelet aggregation and fibrin polymerization.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Procoagulant release -> Systemic microvascular clotting -> Organ ischemia -> Depletion of platelets/clotting factors + Hyperfibrinolysis -> Profuse systemic hemorrhage.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Dual presentation of (1) multiorgan dysfunction (acute kidney injury, shock lung/ARDS, confusion) and (2) widespread bleeding from skin, mucous membranes, surgical wounds, and venipuncture sites.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Multiorgan Dysfunction & Shock: Occurs due to widespread microembolism plugging organ capillaries and aggravating circulatory shock.\n- Profuse Bleeding: Occurs because clotting factors and platelets are \"consumed\" by the widespread clotting process, leaving insufficient procoagulants for normal hemostasis.",
      "Laboratory Findings / Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "- Platelet Count: Severe thrombocytopenia (<150,000/mm³).\n- Plasma Fibrinogen: Hypofibrinogenemia (decreased below normal 200–400 mg%).\n- FDP & D-Dimer: Markedly elevated (Normal FDP: <10 mg/dL).\n- Coagulation Times: Prolonged PT, APTT, and Thrombin Time (TT).",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "Demonstrating elevated D-dimers and urinary/plasma FDPs alongside thrombocytopenia, hypofibrinogenemia, and prolonged PT/APTT/TT.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Treat Underlying Cause: Promptly eliminate the precipitating procoagulant stimulus (e.g., antibiotics for sepsis, evacuation of uterus, surgical debridement).\n- Blood Component Replacement: Transfusion of platelets, fresh frozen plasma, and cryoprecipitate to replenish depleted clotting factors and prevent lethal hemorrhage in bleeding patients.",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Acute severe liver failure, primary hyperfibrinolysis, ITP, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP).",
      "Complications": "Refractory circulatory shock, acute kidney injury (tubular necrosis), Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS / shock lung), and death (septicemic shock mortality >85%)."
