      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "- Venous Thrombosis (e.g., DVT): Localized swelling, pain, warmth, edema, and tenderness in the affected extremity.\n- Arterial Thrombosis: Acute ischemic tissue death (infarction); e.g., severe crushing chest pain in coronary thrombosis (Acute Myocardial Infarction) or sudden neurological deficit in cerebral thrombosis (Stroke).",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Edema & Pain in DVT: Venous occlusion increases upstream capillary hydrostatic pressure, driving fluid transudation into tissue spaces.\n- Infarction: Complete arterial luminal occlusion stops downstream oxygen/nutrient delivery, triggering ischemic cell necrosis.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Doppler Ultrasonography / Echocardiography: Non-invasive visualization of blood flow and intravascular thrombi.\n- D-Dimer Testing: Assesses circulating fibrin cleavage products to evaluate suspected thromboembolism.",
