      "Pathophysiology": "During the first pregnancy, fetal Rh-positive cells do not usually cross the placenta in amounts sufficient to trigger an immune response. However, during delivery and placental separation, a small leak of fetal blood enters maternal circulation, sensitizing the mother to synthesize anti-Rh agglutinins. In subsequent pregnancies, maternal anti-Rh agglutinins (predominantly IgG type) readily cross the placenta into the fetal bloodstream. These IgG antibodies bind to Rh-positive fetal red cells, making them fragile and causing rapid agglutination, intravascular hemolysis, and extravascular phagocytic destruction.",
