      "Definition": "A pathophysiologic state in which an abnormality of cardiac function results in the inability of the heart to pump blood at a rate adequate to satisfy the metabolic requirements of body tissues.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Cardiac output is dynamically regulated by intrinsic mechanisms (Frank-Starling law) and autonomic nerve activity to adjust stroke volume and heart rate according to body tissue needs.",
      "Etiology": "Decreased myocardial contractility (e.g., ischemic heart disease, acute myocardial infarction, cardiomyopathies), pressure overload (severe hypertension, aortic stenosis), volume overload (valvular regurgitation, congenital shunts), or vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency (beriberi).",
      "Site": "Heart muscle (left ventricle, right ventricle, or both) and systemic/pulmonary vascular beds.",
      "Pathophysiology": "- Primary cardiac insult depresses myocardial contractility or imposes excessive pressure/volume loads.\n- Impaired ventricular emptying increases end-systolic and end-diastolic volumes, causing backward damming of blood into venous compartments.\n- Activation of neurohumoral compensatory mechanisms initially sustains arterial pressure, but progressive ventricular dilation increases chamber radius ( r ), which according to Laplace's Law ( T = P x r ) increases myocardial wall tension ( T ), workload, and oxygen consumption, leading to ultimate pump failure.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Reduced ejection fraction, elevated ventricular filling pressures, pulmonary or systemic venous congestion, and impaired tissue hypoperfusion.",
      "Types / Classification": "Acute vs. Chronic, Left-sided vs. Right-sided, High-output vs. Low-output, Forward vs. Backward, Systolic vs. Diastolic.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Myocardial injury/overload -> Fall in cardiac output -> Baroreceptor reflex activation & sympathoadrenal discharge -> RAAS activation & ADH release -> Renal salt and water retention -> Preload increases (Frank-Starling compensation) -> Progressive chamber dilation -> Laplace wall stress increases -> Decompensation -> Pulmonary/systemic edema & tissue hypoxia.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Exertional dyspnea, orthopnea, Paroxysmal Nocturnal Dyspnea (PND), fatigue, dependent edema (ankle/pedal or sacral), tender hepatomegaly, elevated JVP, and ascites.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Dyspnea on Exertion: Left ventricular failure causes pulmonary venous congestion and tissue hypoxia when exercise increases metabolic oxygen demand.\n- Orthopnea (Dyspnea in Recumbent Position): Re-assuming a lying position redistributes blood from the abdomen and lower extremities into the chest, increasing pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure; additionally, the diaphragm is pushed upward by abdominal contents.\n- Paroxysmal Nocturnal Dyspnea (PND): Sudden nighttime episodes of breathlessness and coughing caused by nocturnal depression of respiratory centers combined with gradual transudation of fluid into alveolar air spaces as pulmonary venous/capillary pressures rise during prolonged recumbency.\n- Dependent Edema: Elevated capillary hydrostatic pressure (venous damming) combined with RAAS-mediated salt and water retention and sympathetic renal vasoconstriction decreasing GFR.\n- Elevated JVP & Hepatomegaly: Right ventricular failure causes backward congestion into the right atrium and systemic venous system, elevating central venous pressure and engorging the liver.",
      "Laboratory Findings / Diagnostic Tests": "- Echocardiography: Assesses ejection fraction, chamber volumes, and wall motion.\n- Chest X-ray: Shows cardiomegaly and pulmonary vascular congestion.\n- Biomarkers: Elevated B-type Natriuretic Peptide (BNP) / ANP.",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "- Normal Resting Cardiac Output: ~5 L/min; Normal Ejection Fraction: 55–70%.\n- CCF Values: Reduced cardiac output (< 4 L/min in low-output failure); Ejection fraction reduced (< 40% in systolic failure); JVP elevated (> 4 cm above sternal angle).",
      "Compensation": "- Ventricular Hypertrophy: Concentric (in pressure overload) or Eccentric/Dilation (in volume overload) to normalize wall stress.\n- Sympathetic Activation: Increases heart rate and myocardial contractility.\n- RAAS Activation & ADH Release: Aldosterone and Angiotensin II cause renal sodium and water retention to expand blood volume, increasing end-diastolic volume to maintain stroke volume via the Frank-Starling mechanism.",
      "Decompensation": "Dilation increases chamber radius, drastically increasing wall stress (Laplace Law). When pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure exceeds plasma colloid osmotic pressure (~28 mmHg), acute pulmonary edema ensues, progressing to cardiogenic shock.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Rest & Salt Restriction: Decreases physical demand on the heart and reduces fluid retention.\n- Digitalis (Digoxin): Inhibits Na+-K+ ATPase on myocardial cells -> increases intracellular Na+ -> decreases Na+-Ca2+ exchange -> increases intracellular Ca2+ -> positive inotropic effect boosting contractility.\n- Diuretics (Furosemide): Promote renal excretion of water and salt, decreasing ECF volume, venous return (preload), and cardiac workload.\n- ACE Inhibitors (Captopril, Enalapril) & ARBs (Losartan): Block Angiotensin II synthesis/receptors -> decrease systemic vascular resistance (afterload) and prevent adverse cardiac remodeling.",
      "Drugs": "Digoxin, Furosemide, Captopril, Enalapril, Losartan, Spironolactone, Beta-blockers.",
      "Receptors / Channels / Transporters": "Na+-K+ ATPase pump, Na+-Ca2+ exchanger, AT1 receptor, ACE enzyme, Aldosterone receptor.",
      "Equations / Relationships": "- Laplace's Law: T = P x r (where T = wall tension, P = intraventricular pressure, r = chamber radius).",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Liver cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).",
      "Complications": "Acute pulmonary edema, cardiogenic shock, cardiac arrhythmias (atrial fibrillation), renal failure, and sudden cardiac death."
