      "Etiology": "Accumulation of fluid or blood in the pericardium (hemopericardium) from cardiac trauma, rupture of the heart wall post-myocardial infarction, severe acute pericarditis (viral, tuberculous, or uremic), or malignant pericardial effusions.",
      "Site": "Pericardial sac surrounding the heart, particularly compressing thin-walled right atrium and right ventricle.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Accumulation of fluid within the inelastic parietal pericardium raises intrapericardial pressure. Intrapericardial pressure compresses the heart chambers and restricts ventricular muscle relaxation in diastole. Transmural filling pressure falls, decreasing end-diastolic volume (EDV) and leading to a drop in stroke volume and cardiac output.",
