      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Beck’s Triad (Hypotension, Distant/Muffled heart sounds, Elevated JVP); Pulsus Paradoxus; tachypnea; dyspnea; narrow pulse pressure; sinus tachycardia.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Beck’s Triad (Hypotension, Elevated JVP, Muffled Heart Sounds): Hypotension is caused by diminished stroke volume; elevated JVP results from systemic venous damming behind compressed right chambers; muffled heart sounds occur because the pericardial fluid layer dampens acoustic transmission of valvular closure vibrations.\n- Pulsus Paradoxus: On inspiration, negative intrathoracic pressure increases right ventricular venous return, expanding the right ventricle. In tamponade, the rigid fluid outer boundary forces the interventricular septum to bulge leftward into the left ventricular cavity (interventricular dependence), reducing left ventricular filling, EDV, and systemic pulse volume during inspiration.",
