    "sections": {
      "Name with Synonyms": "Essential Hypertension, Primary Hypertension, Idiopathic Systemic Arterial Hypertension.",
      "Definition": "A chronic cardiovascular condition characterized by persistent, sustained elevation of systemic arterial blood pressure (systolic blood pressure >= 140 mmHg and/or diastolic blood pressure >= 90 mmHg) without an identifiable secondary cause, accounting for ~90% of all clinical hypertension cases.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Systemic arterial blood pressure is determined by Cardiac Output (CO) and Total Peripheral Resistance (TPR). Normal adult blood pressure is SBP < 120 mmHg and DBP < 80 mmHg. Short-term control is mediated by baroreceptors and the sympathetic nervous system; long-term regulation is governed by the renal-body fluid feedback system, RAAS, and natriuretic peptides.",
      "Etiology": "Multifactorial interplay of genetic susceptibility and environmental risk factors:\n- Genetic: Polygenic inheritance (inherited as Mendelian dominant patterns in family lines).\n- Environmental/Lifestyle: Excessive dietary salt intake (salt-sensitivity), obesity, chronic stress (sympathetic overactivity), physical inactivity, cigarette smoking (nicotine vasoconstriction), excessive alcohol intake, advanced age, and post-menopausal hormone changes.",
      "Site": "Systemic resistance arterioles, juxtaglomerular apparatus of kidneys, sympathetic nervous system, and vascular smooth muscle beds.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Pathogenesis involves several physiological disruptions:\n1. Sympathetic Hyperactivity: Increased basal sympathetic discharge increases heart rate, cardiac contractility, and arteriolar constriction (raising TPR).\n2. Renal-Body Fluid Dysregulation: Impaired renal pressure natriuresis resets the resetting threshold, requiring higher arterial pressure to excrete dietary sodium and water.\n3. RAAS Inappropriate Activation: Excess renin/angiotensin II causes direct vasoconstriction and aldosterone-mediated renal sodium retention.\n4. Vascular Structural Remodeling: Chronic high wall stress induces vascular smooth muscle hypertrophy and wall stiffness (decreased arterial compliance), narrowing vessel lumens and permanently elevating baseline TPR.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Elevated total peripheral resistance (TPR), arterial wall stiffness, increased left ventricular afterload, and altered renal pressure-natriuresis setpoint.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Genetic/environmental risk factors -> Sympathetic overactivity + RAAS activation + Reduced renal salt excretion -> Increased cardiac output or arteriolar constriction -> Sustained arterial pressure rise -> Arterial smooth muscle hypertrophy -> Vessel wall stiffness and compliance drop -> Permanent TPR elevation -> Concentric left ventricular hypertrophy + Microvascular damage -> End-organ damage (heart, brain, kidney, retina).",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Often asymptomatic (\"silent killer\"); when symptomatic: occipital morning headaches, dizziness, tinnitus, palpitations, epistaxis, and signs of end-organ involvement (retinal hemorrhages, S4 gallop).",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Occipital Headache: Elevated arterial pressure during early morning circadian surges increases intracranial vascular wall tension.\n- Left Ventricular Hypertrophy (S4 Gallop): Increased afterload forces the left ventricle to pump against higher resistance; concentric hypertrophy develops to normalize wall stress (Laplace Law), reducing ventricular compliance and producing an S4 sound during atrial systole.",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Dipstick proteinuria, microalbuminuria, elevated serum creatinine, hyperuricemia, hyperlipidemia, and impaired fasting glucose.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Blood Pressure Measurement: Standardized sphygmomanometry or Ambulatory Blood Pressure Monitoring (ABPM).\n- Electrocardiogram (ECG): Left Ventricular Hypertrophy (LVH) criteria (S wave in V1 + R wave in V5/V6 > 35 mm; Left Axis Deviation).\n- Fundoscopy: Retinal changes (arteriolar narrowing, AV nicking, cotton wool spots, papilledema).",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "- Normal: SBP 100–119 mmHg and DBP 60–79 mmHg.\n- Prehypertension: SBP 120–139 mmHg or DBP 80–89 mmHg.\n- Stage 1 Hypertension: SBP 140–159 mmHg or DBP 90–99 mmHg.\n- Stage 2 Hypertension: SBP >= 160 mmHg or DBP >= 100 mmHg.\n- Isolated Systolic Hypertension: SBP >= 140 mmHg and DBP < 90 mmHg.",
      "Compensation": "Concentric left ventricular hypertrophy normalizes myocardial wall stress; arterial baroreceptors reset upward within days to accept the higher pressure level as \"normal\".",
      "Decompensation": "Progressive cardiac dilation and heart failure; accelerated atherosclerosis; cerebral thrombosis/hemorrhage; renal arteriolosclerosis leading to chronic kidney disease.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Lifestyle Modifications: Weight loss (1 kg loss reduces SBP by ~2 mmHg), salt restriction (< 6 g/day), regular exercise, smoking cessation.\n- Diuretics (Thiazides): Promote renal sodium/water excretion, lowering ECF volume and long-term TPR.\n- ACE Inhibitors (Captopril, Enalapril) & ARBs (Losartan): Block conversion/action of Angiotensin II on AT1 receptors -> induce arteriolar vasodilation, prevent aldosterone secretion, and retard vascular/cardiac remodeling.\n- Calcium Channel Blockers (Amlodipine, Verapamil, Diltiazem): Inhibit calcium entry into cells by interacting with the alpha-1 subunit of L-type voltage-dependent calcium channels -> decrease vascular tone and TPR.\n- Beta-Blockers (Atenolol, Metoprolol): Block cardiac Beta-1 receptors (lower HR and CO) and renal Beta-1 receptors (inhibit renin release).",
      "Drugs": "Hydrochlorothiazide, Enalapril, Losartan, Amlodipine, Verapamil, Diltiazem, Atenolol.",
      "Receptors / Channels / Transporters": "L-type Calcium channels, AT1 receptors, Alpha-1 and Beta-1 adrenergic receptors, Na+-Cl- cotransporter (in DCT).",
      "Equations / Relationships": "Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) = Cardiac Output (CO) x Total Peripheral Resistance (TPR); MAP = Diastolic BP + 1/3 Pulse Pressure; Laplace Law: Wall Stress = (Pressure x Radius) / (2 x Wall Thickness).",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Secondary hypertension (renal parenchymal disease, renovascular stenosis, pheochromocytoma, Cushing’s, hyperaldosteronism), White-coat hypertension.",
      "Complications": "Hypertensive heart disease (LVH, HF), Coronary Artery Disease (AMI), Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms causing stroke (cerebral hemorrhage/infarction), Hypertensive Encephalopathy, Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis (CKD), Retinopathy."
    }
