    "sections": {
      "Name with Synonyms": "Orthostatic Hypotension, Postural Hypotension, Orthostatic Vascular Collapse, Postural Orthostatic Dysautonomia.",
      "Definition": "A form of cardiovascular dysregulation defined as a sustained reduction in arterial blood pressure—specifically a fall in Systolic Blood Pressure of >= 20 mmHg and/or Diastolic Blood Pressure of >= 10 mmHg—occurring within 3 minutes of assuming an upright/standing position from a supine posture.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Upon standing, gravity causes pooling of 500–1000 mL of blood in the venous capacitance vessels of the lower extremities and splanchnic circulation. This temporary drop in venous return lowers EDV, stroke volume, and arterial blood pressure. Normally, this unloads arterial baroreceptors (carotid sinus and aortic arch), triggering a rapid reflex response: sympathetic outflow increases while parasympathetic (vagal) tone decreases. This results in reflex tachycardia (HR increases by 10–20 bpm) and arteriolar/venous vasoconstriction, immediately restoring blood pressure to normal within seconds.",
      "Etiology": "1. Acute Postural Hypotension (Transient):\n- Prolonged standing or immobilization.\n- Getting up from bed after a prolonged illness.\n- Post-strenuous physical exercise (cutaneous vasodilation).\n- Severe hypovolemia/dehydration (diarrhea, vomiting, hemorrhage, excessive diuresis).\n2. Chronic Primary Postural Hypotension:\n- Idiopathic Postural Hypotension / Primary Autonomic Failure (e.g., Pure Autonomic Failure, Multiple System Atrophy) due to degeneration of peripheral autonomic nerves in the elderly.\n3. Chronic Secondary Postural Hypotension:\n- Diabetic Autonomic Neuropathy (most common).\n- Neurodegenerative / Infectious: Amyloidosis, Syringomyelia, Tabes Dorsalis, Subacute Combined Degeneration, Beriberi.\n- Pharmacological: Sympatholytic drugs, vasodilator therapy, antihypertensives, surgical sympathectomy.",
      "Site": "Baroreceptor reflex arc, rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM), sympathetic pre/postganglionic fibers, and vascular smooth muscle.",
      "Pathophysiology": "A defect or interruption in the baroreceptor reflex arc or autonomic sympathetic output prevents compensatory vasoconstriction and heart rate increase upon standing. Gravity-induced venous pooling in lower limbs decreases venous return, cardiac filling, EDV, and cardiac output. Without autonomic sympathetic compensation, systemic arterial blood pressure falls sharply, reducing cerebral perfusion pressure below the autoregulatory range.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Failure of reflex sympathetic vasoconstriction and reflex tachycardia on orthostatic stress, causing transient cerebral ischemia and postural syncope.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Assumption of upright position -> Gravitational pooling of blood in lower limbs -> Reduced venous return -> Decreased EDV and Stroke Volume -> Acute drop in arterial BP -> Autonomic nerve failure -> Inability to constrict resistance vessels or increase HR -> Sustained fall in SBP >= 20 or DBP >= 10 mmHg -> Acute cerebral hypoperfusion -> Giddiness, blurred vision, and syncope -> Falling to supine position restores venous return -> Consciousness regained.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Postural lightheadedness, giddiness, dizziness, weakness, lethargy, blurred vision, and transient fainting/syncope upon standing up.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Lightheadedness, Blurred Vision & Syncope: Gravity-induced BP drop leads to acute reduction in cerebral blood flow when arterial BP falls below the lower cerebral autoregulatory limit.\n- Absence of Tachycardia (in Neurogenic/Autonomic Failure): Interruption of sympathetic cardiac accelerator fibers prevents compensatory heart rate elevation despite severe blood pressure drop (fixed heart rate).",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Fasting plasma glucose and HbA1c (to check for diabetic neuropathy), serum cobalamin/B12, plasma norepinephrine levels (low supine or failure to rise on standing in neurogenic forms).",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Posture Blood Pressure Test: Measuring SBP and DBP in supine position and after 1 and 3 minutes of standing.\n- Head-Up Tilt (HUT) Table Test: Passive tilting to 60–70 degrees while monitoring continuous BP and ECG; positive if SBP falls >= 20 mmHg or DBP >= 10 mmHg.\n- Autonomic Function Tests (AFTs): Valsalva ratio, heart rate response to deep breathing (E:I ratio), isometric handgrip test, Standing-to-Lying ratio (30:15 ratio), Cold Pressor Test.",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "- Normal Orthostatic Response: SBP changes < 10 mmHg, DBP changes < 5 mmHg, HR increases by 10–20 bpm.\n- Orthostatic Hypotension Definition: Sustained fall in SBP >= 20 mmHg or DBP >= 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing.\n- Severe Orthostatic Fall: Sustained fall in SBP > 40 mmHg or DBP > 20 mmHg.",
      "Compensation": "Gravitational fall to a supine position (fainting) mechanically brings the body horizontal, eliminating the hydrostatic gravity column, returning pooled venous blood to the heart, and immediately restoring cardiac output and cerebral perfusion.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Non-Pharmacological: Gradual position changes, abdominal binders and elastic compression stockings (prevent lower extremity venous pooling), increased salt and fluid intake (expands ECF volume), avoiding hot environments/baths.\n- Fludrocortisone: Synthetic mineralocorticoid that enhances renal distal tubular sodium reabsorption, expanding plasma volume and increasing vascular reactivity to vasopressors.\n- Midodrine: Selective Alpha-1 adrenergic receptor agonist that directly constricts arteriolar and venous smooth muscle, increasing total peripheral resistance and venous return.\n- Pyridostigmine: Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that enhances ganglionic cholinergic transmission in autonomic pathways during orthostatic stress.",
      "Drugs": "Fludrocortisone, Midodrine, Pyridostigmine.",
      "Receptors / Channels / Transporters": "Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors, Mineralocorticoid receptors (MR), Nicotinic and Muscarinic acetylcholine receptors.",
      "Equations / Relationships": "Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP) = Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) - Intracranial Pressure (ICP). When standing MAP drops below 60 mmHg, CPP falls below the autoregulatory threshold.",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Vasovagal (Neurocardiogenic) Syncope (has prominent prodromal nausea, diaphoresis, hyper-vagally mediated bradycardia), Postural Orthostatic Tachycardia Syndrome (POTS - marked HR increase > 30 bpm without significant BP fall), Hypovolemic Shock.",
      "Complications": "Traumatic head injuries and fractures from falls, ischemic stroke, acute myocardial ischemia, dementia/cognitive decline from recurrent cerebral hypoperfusion."
    }
