    "name": "Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)",
    "tab": "RESPIRATORY SYSTEM",
    "sections": {
      "Name with Synonyms": "Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD), Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease.",
      "Definition": "A complex group of chronic obstructive conditions—primarily comprising chronic bronchitis and emphysema—characterized by progressive, long-term obstruction of airflow in the respiratory tract.",
      "Normal Physiology": "In healthy lungs, air flows down conducting airways via bulk pressure gradients, and normal elastic recoil of the lung parenchyma allows passive expiration without significant airway resistance.",
      "Etiology": "Chronic habitual cigarette smoking and long-term inhalation of irritating air pollutants or smoke.",
      "Site": "Small bronchioles, bronchial walls, alveolar septa, and pulmonary capillary beds.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Chronic smoke exposure paralyzes respiratory epithelial cilia (mediated by nicotine), inhibits alveolar macrophages, and stimulates excess mucus secretion alongside inflammatory edema of bronchiolar epithelium. Chronic small airway obstruction leads to expiratory air trapping, destruction of alveolar septal walls, loss of pulmonary capillaries, and severe ventilation-perfusion ( V_A/Q ) mismatching.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Markedly elevated airway resistance, greatly increased work of breathing (especially during expiration), reduced pulmonary diffusing capacity, abnormal V_A/Q ratios (producing both physiological shunts and physiological dead spaces), and increased pulmonary vascular resistance leading to pulmonary hypertension.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Inhalation of tobacco smoke -> Ciliary paralysis and macrophage inhibition -> Mucus hypersecretion and bronchiolar mucosal edema -> Chronic small airway obstruction -> Alveolar air trapping during expiration -> Destruction of alveolar septa and capillary beds -> V_A/Q mismatch and elevated pulmonary vascular resistance -> Hypoxia, hypercapnia, and right-sided heart failure.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Severe, prolonged **\"air hunger\" (dyspnea)**, chronic productive cough, cyanosis, and exercise intolerance.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Air Hunger / Dyspnea: Driven by excessive accumulation of CO_2 (hypercapnia) and hypoxia in body fluids, combined with the immense work performed by respiratory muscles to overcome airflow resistance.\n- Hypoxia and Hypercapnia: Arise from alveolar hypoventilation, loss of respiratory membrane diffusing surface area, and severe V_A/Q mismatching.\n- Mixed Cyanosis: Results from chronic cor pulmonale where inadequate arterial oxygenation (central component) combines with sluggish peripheral capillary circulation (peripheral component), elevating deoxygenated hemoglobin above 5 g/dL.",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Hypoxemia (low PaO_2 ), hypercapnia (high PaCO_2 ), elevated plasma bicarbonate ( HCO_3- ) in compensated respiratory acidosis, and secondary polycythemia.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "Timed spirometry with forced expiratory maneuvers, arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis, carbon monoxide diffusing capacity ( DL_CO ), and residual volume (RV) / functional residual capacity (FRC) measurement by helium dilution or body plethysmography.",
      "Graphs / Tracings": "- Spirometry ( FEV_1 curve): Shows a prolonged, gentle expiratory slope with FEV_1 significantly reduced relative to FVC.\n- Flow-Volume Loop: Demonstrates a reduced peak expiratory flow (PEF) and a concave, \"scooped-out\" expiratory curve shifted to higher lung volumes.\n- Dynamic Pressure-Volume Loop: Shows an expanded loop area representing increased energy expended to overcome flow resistance.",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "Normal FEV_1/FVC ratio is 0.75–0.80 (75–80%), whereas in COPD it drops below 70%. Normal PaO_2 is 85–95 mmHg and PaCO_2 is 36–44 mmHg; in COPD hypoventilation, PaO_2 is < 80 mmHg and PaCO_2 exceeds 45 mmHg. TLC, RV, and FRC are abnormally increased.",
      "Compensation": "The kidneys retain HCO_3- and excrete H+ ions over several days to compensate for chronic respiratory acidosis. Chronic tissue hypoxia stimulates erythropoietin secretion, raising red cell mass (polycythemia).",
      "Decompensation": "Type II respiratory failure, profound uncompensated respiratory acidosis, severe pulmonary hypertension, right ventricular hypertrophy, and right-sided heart failure (cor pulmonale).",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "Low-concentration controlled oxygen therapy to relieve severe tissue hypoxia without abolishing the peripheral chemoreceptor hypoxic drive. Bronchodilators to relax bronchiolar smooth muscle and reduce expiratory work of breathing.",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Differentiated from restrictive lung diseases (e.g., pulmonary fibrosis), where lung volumes (TLC, FVC) are reduced but the FEV_1/FVC ratio remains normal or elevated.",
      "Complications": "Chronic cor pulmonale (right heart failure), secondary polycythemia, type II respiratory failure, and permanent pulmonary disability."
    }
