      "Name with Synonyms": "Cyanosis.",
      "Definition": "Bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes of the body resulting from the presence of reduced hemoglobin (deoxyhemoglobin) in excess of 5 g/dL (5 g%) in capillary blood, or from excess abnormal hemoglobin derivatives.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Blood color is determined by the oxygenation state of hemoglobin; oxyhemoglobin is bright red, whereas reduced hemoglobin is dark blue-purple. Cyanosis is typically clinically detectable when arterial oxygen saturation drops below 85%.",
      "Etiology": "- Central Cyanosis: Inadequate oxygenation of systemic arterial blood (hypoxic hypoxia) or right-to-left shunting of blood. Caused by low atmospheric oxygen (high altitude), hypoventilation (airway obstruction, lung collapse), impaired gas diffusion (hyaline membrane disease), or congenital heart diseases with right-to-left shunts (Tetralogy of Fallot, patent truncus arteriosus, transposition of great vessels, pulmonary arteriovenous fistula).\n- Peripheral Cyanosis: Sluggish peripheral circulation leading to excess tissue oxygen extraction (stagnant hypoxia). Caused by decreased cardiac output (heart failure), local vasoconstriction (extreme cold), venous obstruction (superior vena cava syndrome), increased blood viscosity (polycythemia, multiple myeloma), or arterial obstruction (Buerger disease, Raynaud disease).\n- Mixed Cyanosis: Combined central and peripheral mechanisms, as seen in chronic cor pulmonale due to emphysema, cardiogenic shock with pulmonary edema, or severe congestive cardiac failure.\n- Other Subtypes: Differential cyanosis (cyanosis limited to lower limbs, as in patent ductus arteriosus with reversed shunt secondary to pulmonary hypertension); Intermittent cyanosis (Ebstein anomaly); Cyclical cyanosis (bilateral choanal atresia); Orthocyanosis (cyanosis occurring only in the upright posture, seen in pulmonary arteriovenous malformations).",
