      "Definition": "A respiratory sleep disorder defined by repeated episodes of spontaneous cessation of airflow (apnea lasting 10 seconds or longer) occurring 300 to 500 times per night during sleep.",
      "Types / Classification": "1. Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA): Most common; caused by mechanical blockage/closure of the upper airway (especially the pharynx) due to marked relaxation of pharyngeal dilator muscles during sleep, particularly during REM sleep. Contributed to by obesity (excess fat deposition in soft tissues of the pharynx/neck), enlarged tonsils or adenoids, macroglossia (large tongue), or nasal obstruction.\n2. Central Sleep Apnea: Caused by transient cessation of neural output from the medullary respiratory center to the phrenic nerves and respiratory muscles. Occurs in strokes, CNS infections/infarctions, or structural brainstem damage that decreases respiratory center sensitivity to CO2 and H+.",
