      "Regulation / Feedback": "In health, gastric acid autoregulates gastrin release from antral G cells. In chronic atrophic gastritis with parietal cell loss and achlorhydria, the absence of acidic negative feedback causes compensatory hypergastrinemia (feedback elevation of serum gastrin).",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Mucosal barrier breakdown -> Increased permeability to H+ -> H+ back-diffusion into epithelium -> Local inflammation & cell death -> Mucosal atrophy -> Loss of parietal & chief cells -> Achlorhydria & intrinsic factor deficiency -> Megaloblastic/Pernicious anemia.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Upper abdominal discomfort, indigestion/dyspepsia, epigastric fullness, nausea, and vomiting. In chronic atrophic cases: fatigue, pallor, and glossitis due to anemia.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Dyspepsia & Epigastric Pain: Back-diffusion of acid and inflammatory mediators stimulate submucosal pain fibers in the gastric wall.\n- Anemia / Fatigue: Destruction of parietal cells leads to loss of intrinsic factor (preventing Vitamin B_12 absorption and causing megaloblastic/pernicious anemia) and loss of HCl (preventing Fe3+ to Fe2+ reduction, causing microcytic iron deficiency anemia).",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Macrocytic normochromic red cells with hypersegmented neutrophils (pernicious anemia) or microcytic hypochromic red cells (iron deficiency); low serum Vitamin B_12; low serum ferritin.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "Upper GI Endoscopy (gastroscopy) with mucosal biopsy; Gastric Juice Analysis (BAO/MAO evaluation via pentagastrin or histamine test showing achlorhydria/hypochlorhydria); Schilling test (evaluating Vitamin B_12 /intrinsic factor absorption).",
