      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Nausea and vomiting, abdominal pain/cramps, inability to pass flatus or stool (obstipation), marked abdominal distension, **silent abdomen** (complete absence of normal bowel sounds on auscultation), hyper-resonant percussion note, and rebound tenderness/guarding if peritonitis is present.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Silent Abdomen: Total absence of peristaltic propulsive movements moving luminal contents.\n- Abdominal Distension & Hyper-resonance: Trapped gas and fluid expanding intestinal loops.\n- Vomiting: Retrograde accumulation and antiperistaltic reflux of unpropelled intestinal juices back into the stomach.\n- Inability to Pass Flatus/Stools: Functional obstruction preventing forward transit to the rectum.",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Serum electrolyte imbalances (marked hypokalemia); leukocytosis (if peritonitis/infection is present); elevated BUN/creatinine (prerenal azotemia from fluid sequestration).",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "Abdominal Auscultation (confirming a silent abdomen); Plain Abdominal X-ray (showing generalized gas-fluid levels throughout dilated loops of small and large bowel without a mechanical transition point); Abdominal CT scan.",
