      "Definition": "A distressing clinical syndrome characterized by gastrointestinal and vasomotor symptoms (weakness, dizziness, sweating, palpitations) developing after a meal in individuals who have undergone gastrectomy or gastrojejunostomy.",
      "Normal Physiology": "The stomach acts as a reservoir regulating the slow, controlled release of chyme into the duodenum (\"pyloric pump\" squirts of 1–5 mL). Neural (enterogastric reflex) and hormonal mechanisms (CCK, secretin, GIP) monitor duodenal osmolality, acidity, and fat content to slow gastric emptying.",
      "Etiology": "Surgical procedures that alter or bypass the pyloric sphincter or stomach reservoir: partial or total gastrectomy, gastrojejunostomy, pyloroplasty, or truncal vagotomy without pyloric control.",
      "Site": "Stomach remnant, duodenum, jejunum, and systemic circulation.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Dumping syndrome arises from two distinct pathophysiological mechanisms Operating after meals:\n1. Hypovolemic / Osmotic Mechanism (Early Dumping, within 15–30 min):\n  Loss of pyloric regulation causes rapid, unrestrained \"dumping\" of a hypertonic food bolus directly into the jejunum. The high osmotic load draws large volumes of water from blood into the intestinal lumen, causing acute intravascular hypovolemia, dehydration, hypotension, and splanchnic distension.\n2. Hypoglycemic Mechanism (Late Dumping, ~2 hours post-meal):\n  Rapid delivery and swift intestinal absorption of carbohydrates cause a sudden spike in blood glucose (hyperglycemia). This triggers an exaggerated, surge release of insulin from pancreatic beta cells. Excess insulin rapidly drives glucose into cells, producing severe reactive hypoglycemia.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Abnormally accelerated gastric emptying, loss of pyloric resistance, intestinal hyperosmolality, acute plasma volume shifts, and hyperinsulinemic reactive hypoglycemia.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Gastrectomy/bypass -> Ingestion of meal -> Rapid delivery of hypertonic chyme to jejunum -> High luminal osmotic pressure -> Shift of water from blood into gut -> Plasma hypovolemia & hypotension -> Early weakness & dizziness -> Simultaneous rapid glucose absorption -> Hyperglycemic spike -> Excessive insulin release -> Late reactive hypoglycemia -> Late sweating, tremors, & dizziness.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "- Early Symptoms (15–30 min post-meal): Epigastric fullness, abdominal cramps, borborygmi, diarrhea, facial flushing, weakness, dizziness, and lightheadedness.\n- Late Symptoms (~2 hours post-meal): Sweating, tremors, palpitations, hunger, lightheadedness, weakness, and syncope.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Early Dizziness & Weakness: Intravascular fluid shift into the gut lumen produces acute hypovolemia and systemic hypotension.\n- Late Sweating & Tremors: Severe reactive hypoglycemia activates sympathoadrenal medullary discharge (epinephrine release) to elevate blood glucose.\n- Borborygmi & Diarrhea: Intestinal fluid transudation and rapid intestinal transit.",
      "Laboratory Findings": "- Early: Transient hemoconcentration (elevated hematocrit).\n- Late: Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT) demonstrates an initial steep hyperglycemic peak (> 200 mg/dL) at 30–60 min followed by severe hypoglycemia (< 50 mg/dL) at 2 hours, with hyperinsulinemia.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "Scintigraphic Gastric Emptying Study (demonstrating abnormally rapid solid/liquid emptying rates); Oral Glucose Tolerance Test.",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "Normal solid gastric emptying T_1/2 = 60--120 minutes; in dumping syndrome, T_1/2 is markedly shortened (< 30 minutes). Postprandial blood glucose drops below 50 mg/dL during late dumping.",
      "Compensation": "Sympathetic stimulation releases catecholamines to compensate for early hypotension and late hypoglycemia.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Dietary Modifications: Consume small, frequent, dry meals; restrict refined simple carbohydrates (prevents hypertonicity and insulin spikes); separate solid food and liquid intake by 30 minutes; increase dietary protein and fat (fats stimulate CCK/GIP release, which naturally slows GI transit).\n- Pharmacotherapy: Somatostatin analogs (octreotide) to inhibit gastroenteropancreatic hormone release (insulin, VIP), slow gastric emptying, and reduce splanchnic blood flow.\n- Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors (Acarbose): Delay intestinal carbohydrate breakdown and absorption, blunting postprandial glucose and insulin surges.",
      "Drugs": "Octreotide (somatostatin analog), Acarbose.",
      "Receptors / Channels / Transporters": "SGLT1 and GLUT2 (glucose absorption), Somatostatin SSTR_2/SSTR_5 receptors, Insulin receptors.",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Postprandial vasovagal syncope, insulinoma, primary autonomic neuropathy, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.",
      "Complications": "Severe weight loss, malnutrition, chronic diarrhea, fear of eating (phagophobia), and debilitating hypoglycemic syncope."
