      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Chronic diarrhea, **steatorrhea** (pale, voluminous, loose, bulky, foul-smelling stools containing excess fatty acid salts), abdominal distension, cramps, weight loss/body wasting, osteomalacia, bleeding tendencies, and anemia.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Steatorrhea: Impaired mucosal absorption of digested fats; unabsorbed fatty acids form salts in the stool, drawing water osmosis-wise into the lumen.\n- Body Wasting / Weight Loss: Severe calorie and protein malabsorption.\n- Osteomalacia: Impaired calcium and Vitamin D absorption leading to bone demineralization.\n- Bleeding Tendencies (Ecchymosis/Purpura): Malabsorption of fat-soluble Vitamin K impairing hepatic synthesis of clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X).\n- Anemia: Dual malabsorption of iron (microcytic anemia) in the duodenum/jejunum and folic acid/Vitamin B_12 (macrocytic anemia) in the ileum.",
      "Laboratory Findings": "- Serology: Positive Anti-tissue Transglutaminase (anti-tTG IgA), Anti-Endomysial antibodies (EMA), and Anti-Deamidated Gliadin Peptides (DGP).\n- Stool Analysis: Fecal fat excretion > 7 g/24 hours (normal < 5 g/day).\n- Hematology: Anemia (low Hb, low serum ferritin, or macrocytosis); prolonged Prothrombin Time (PT) due to Vitamin K deficiency; hypocalcemia.",
