      "Sequence of Changes": "Neural crest cell migration failure → Distal colonic aganglionosis → Persistent spastic contraction of distal segment → Obstruction to fecal outflow → Mass accumulation of feces proximal to obstruction → Progressive distension of normal proximal colon (megacolon up to 3–4 inches in diameter).",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Severe, obstinate constipation (bowel movements occurring only once every several days or weeks), marked abdominal distension, failure or delay in passing meconium in newborns (appearing as early as the 3rd day after birth), anorexia, vomiting, and lassitude.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Abdominal Distension & Constipation: Continuous accumulation of feces and trapped gas proximal to the non-relaxing aganglionic segment inflates the proximal colon.\n- Failure to Pass Meconium: The non-innervated internal anal sphincter remains tightly closed despite rectal filling.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Anorectal Manometry: Demonstrates the absence of reflex relaxation of the internal anal sphincter upon rectal balloon distension.\n- Rectal Biopsy: Gold standard; demonstrates complete absence of ganglion cells in the submucosa and myenteric layer along with hypertrophied nerve fibers.\n- Barium Enema: Reveals a narrow, spastic distal segment with a cone-shaped transition zone leading into a massively dilated proximal colon.",
