      "Name with Synonyms": "Pancreatitis (Acute Pancreatitis, Chronic Pancreatitis, Hemorrhagic Pancreatitis).",
      "Definition": "An inflammatory disease of the pancreas characterized by premature intracellular activation of proteolytic pancreatic digestive enzymes within the acini and ducts, resulting in autodigestion of pancreatic tissue and surrounding structures.",
      "Normal Physiology": "The exocrine pancreas secretes 1.0 to 1.5 liters per day of alkaline (pH 7.5–8.5), enzyme-rich fluid containing trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, lipase, and amylase. Autodigestion is normally prevented by: (1) synthesizing enzymes as inactive proenzymes (zymogens), (2) co-secreting trypsin inhibitor in acinar cells, and (3) maintaining thick acinar cell walls. Trypsinogen is normally activated only upon reaching the small intestine by brush border enteropeptidase (enterokinase).",
      "Etiology": "Obstruction of the papilla of Vater by gallstones, chronic alcohol abuse (the two together accounting for >75% of cases), hypertriglyceridemia, blunt abdominal trauma, hypercalcemia, and drugs (azathioprine, sulfonamides, tetracycline, valproic acid).",
      "Site": "Pancreatic acini, pancreatic ductal system, retroperitoneal adipose tissue, and abdominal cavity.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Blockage of pancreatic ducts or acinar cell trauma causes pooling of pancreatic juice. Accumulation of trypsinogen eventually overcomes the protective capacity of trypsin inhibitor, initiating autocatalytic conversion of trypsinogen into active trypsin within the gland. Active trypsin then activates chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, proelastase, and phospholipase A2. Phospholipase A2 converts lecithin into lysolecithin, damaging cell membranes and producing extensive fat necrosis. Elastase digests blood vessel walls, producing severe intra-abdominal hemorrhage (hemorrhagic pancreatitis).",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Loss of pancreatic ductular integrity, autodigestive tissue necrosis, massive fluid transudation into retroperitoneal/peritoneal spaces, and progressive loss of exocrine/endocrine function.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Duct obstruction / alcohol toxicity → Pooled zymogen secretions → Trypsin inhibitor capacity overwhelmed → Intra-pancreatic trypsinogen activation → Cascade activation of elastase & phospholipase A2 → Autodigestion, cell necrosis, & fat necrosis → Vascular erosion & intra-abdominal hemorrhage → Systemic inflammatory response & circulatory shock.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Severe, agonizing epigastric or periumbilical pain that characteristically radiates straight through to the back, nausea, vomiting, abdominal distension, fever, tachycardia, hypotension/shock, and in chronic forms, steatorrhea, weight loss, and diabetes mellitus.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Epigastric Pain Radiating to Back: Autodigestive necrosis and inflammatory edema distend the retroperitoneal pancreatic capsule, directly stimulating celiac plexus sensory fibers.\n- Hypotension & Circulatory Shock: Exudation of massive volumes of plasma/blood into the retroperitoneum and peritoneal cavity caused by proteolytic vascular endothelial damage.\n- Steatorrhea (in Chronic Pancreatitis): Destruction of >90% of lipase-secreting acinar tissue leads to unabsorbed fat passing into stool.",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Elevated serum amylase and serum lipase; leukocytosis; hypocalcemia (calcium sequestered in saponified fat necrosis); hyperglycemia; elevated C-reactive protein.",
