      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "- In Children (Panhypopituitary Dwarfism): **Proportionate short stature / dwarfism** (all physical body parts develop in appropriate proportion to one another, but growth rate is greatly decreased), failure to pass through puberty, juvenile childlike physical features, retained high-pitched voice, normal mental development.\n- In Adults (Simmonds Disease / Sheehan Syndrome): Severe lethargy, fatigue, pallor (waxy skin), cold intolerance (hypothyroidism), failure of lactation post-delivery (lack of prolactin in Sheehan syndrome), loss of pubic and axillary hair, amenorrhea, loss of libido/impotence, weight gain, postural hypotension, and hypoglycemia.",
