      "Definition": "The inappropriate, spontaneous, or persistent execution of milk secretion from the breast unassociated with childbirth, nursing, or the normal postpartum lactational state.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Lactogenesis requires two distinct hormonal steps: (1) **Milk Synthesis:** Prolactin stimulates lobuloalveolar epithelial cells in the mammary gland to synthesize casein, lactose, and milk lipids; (2) **Milk Ejection:** Oxytocin released from the posterior pituitary in response to tactile nipple stimulation contracts myoepithelial cells surrounding alveoli to squeeze milk into lactiferous ducts. During pregnancy, high circulating estrogen and progesterone antagonize prolactin's action on milk secretion; post-parturition, the precipitous drop in steroid levels allows unopposed prolactin-driven lactogenesis.",
      "Etiology": "- Hyperprolactinemic Causes: Prolactinoma, dopamine antagonist drugs, primary hypothyroidism, pituitary stalk lesions, chronic renal failure.\n- Normoprolactinemic / Mechanical Causes: Chronic mechanical chest wall stimulation (burns, herpes zoster, thoracotomy scars, frequent self-examination) driving neural reflex arcs; Chiari-Frommel syndrome; McCune-Albright syndrome.\n- Idiopathic: Increased intrinsic breast tissue sensitivity to normal prolactin levels.",
