      "Functional Abnormality": "Non-puerperal stimulation of lactogenesis and continuous milk release.",
      "Regulation / Feedback": "Non-puerperal lactogenesis is normally prevented by tonic dopamine inhibition of prolactin and low basal prolactin levels. Unopposed prolactin action bypasses normal lactational regulation.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Elevated prolactin / chest wall reflex -> Activation of mammary epithelial prolactin receptors -> JAK2/STAT activation -> Synthesis of casein, lactose, & lipids -> Accumulation of milk in lactiferous ducts -> Spontaneous discharge of white/milky fluid from nipple.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Spontaneous or expressible white, milky fluid discharge from one or both breasts (unrelated to recent pregnancy), frequently accompanied by amenorrhea, oligomenorrhea, or head pain/visual changes if caused by a pituitary tumor.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Milky Nipple Discharge: Presence of true milk components (fat globules, lactose, casein) synthesized by alveolar cells under prolactin stimulation.\n- Co-existing Amenorrhea: High prolactin simultaneously inhibits hypothalamic GnRH release, blocking ovulation.",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Microscopic examination of discharge confirming fat globules and absence of blood; serum prolactin level (elevated or normal); elevated TSH (if hypothyroid); pregnancy test (negative hCG).",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Discharge Microscopy / Chemistry: Differentiates true milky galactorrhea (fat droplets on Sudan stain) from bloody or purulent discharge (suspicious for intraductal papilloma or breast carcinoma).\n- Serum Prolactin Assay: Determines if galactorrhea is hyperprolactinemic or normoprolactinemic.\n- Serum TSH and hCG: Rules out primary hypothyroidism and occult pregnancy.",
