      "Name with Synonyms": "Hyperthyroidism, Thyrotoxicosis (broad term for excess thyroid hormone state), Hyperthyroid State.",
      "Definition": "A hypermetabolic clinical state resulting from excessive secretion or action of thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) in body tissues.",
      "Normal Physiology": "The thyroid gland secretes T4 (~93%) and T3 (~7%). T4 is converted peripherally to T3 by deiodinase enzymes (D1, D2). T3 enters cells via carrier-mediated transport and binds to nuclear thyroid hormone receptors (TR alpha and TR beta heterodimerized with RXR at thyroid hormone response elements), increasing gene transcription for Na+-K+ ATPase, uncoupling proteins, beta-adrenergic receptors, and respiratory/mitochondrial enzymes.",
      "Etiology": "- Primary: Graves' disease (most common cause), toxic multinodular goiter, toxic adenoma, activating TSH receptor mutations, excessive iodine intake (Jod-Basedow effect).\n- Secondary: TSH-secreting anterior pituitary adenoma (thyrotroph tumor).\n- Extrathyroidal / Factitious: Exogenous administration of excess thyroid hormone (thyrotoxicosis factitia), hCG-secreting tumors (choriocarcinoma), or lingual/ectopic thyroid.",
      "Site": "Thyroid gland (primary), anterior pituitary gland (secondary), or systemic tissues throughout the body.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Excess T3 and T4 accelerate basal metabolic rate (BMR) by 30% to 100% above normal, increase tissue oxygen consumption, promote uncoupled oxidative phosphorylation (generating excessive body heat), upregulate beta-adrenergic receptor expression and sensitivity to catecholamines, increase muscle protein catabolism, and accelerate glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, and lipolysis.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Generalized hypermetabolism, hypersympathetic state, hyperdynamic circulation, increased gastrointestinal motility, and muscle protein wasting.",
      "Regulation / Feedback": "Elevated free T3 and T4 exert negative feedback on hypothalamic TRH and anterior pituitary thyrotrophs, suppressing TSH secretion to near zero in primary hyperthyroidism.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Autonomous thyroid hypersecretion / stimulation -> Elevated circulating free T3/T4 -> Suppression of pituitary TSH -> Upregulation of tissue BMR and beta-adrenergic receptors -> Hyperdynamic cardiac output, heat intolerance, weight loss, fine tremor, and hyperdefecation.",
