      "Site": "Ovaries (theca and granulosa cells), anterior pituitary, hypothalamus, and peripheral adipose tissue.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Increased GnRH pulse frequency preferentially drives anterior pituitary LH hypersecretion over FSH. High LH acts on ovarian theca cells to upregulate androgen synthesis (androstenedione and testosterone). Relative FSH deficiency impairs granulosa cell aromatase activity, preventing conversion of androgens to estrogens. Arrested antral follicles accumulate beneath a thickened ovarian capsule, producing multiple small subcortical cysts. Peripheral conversion of excess androgens to estrone in adipose tissue maintains tonic low-grade estrogen levels, which further suppresses pituitary FSH while stimulating LH in a vicious feed-forward loop. Hyperinsulinemia secondary to insulin resistance acts synergistically with LH on theca cells to boost androgen production and inhibits hepatic sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG) synthesis, increasing free active testosterone.",
