      "Sequence of Changes": "Hypothalamic GnRH pulse frequency increase -> Pituitary LH hypersecretion with relative FSH deficit -> Ovarian theca cell hyperplasia & hyperandrogenism -> Follicular arrest & polycystic morphology -> Anovulation & oligomenorrhea/amenorrhea -> Adipose aromatization to estrone + Insulin resistance -> Sustained hyperandrogenic state.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Oligomenorrhea or secondary amenorrhea**, **hirsutism** (male-pattern facial/body hair), **acne**, male-pattern alopecia, obesity (central/visceral), acanthosis nigricans, and anovulatory infertility.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Oligomenorrhea / Amenorrhea: Lack of dominant follicle selection prevents the mid-cycle LH surge and subsequent progesterone withdrawal, keeping the endometrium in a persistent, unstable proliferative state.\n- Hirsutism & Acne: High circulating free testosterone and dihydrotestosterone (DHT) bind androgen receptors in pilosebaceous units, stimulating terminal hair growth and sebaceous gland sebum production.\n- Acanthosis Nigricans: Hyperinsulinemia stimulates epidermal keratinocytes and dermal fibroblasts through insulin and IGF-1 receptors, causing hyperpigmented skin plaques.\n- Infertility: Absence of follicular rupture and oocyte release (anovulation).",
