      "Normal Physiology": "Normal pregnancy involves extravillous trophoblast invasion that remodels high-resistance uterine spiral arteries into large-caliber, low-resistance conduits. Progesterone and endothelial nitric oxide/prostacyclin cause systemic vasodilation, resulting in low vascular resistance and a normal or decreased diastolic blood pressure.",
      "Etiology": "Defective or incomplete trophoblastic invasion of spiral arteries leading to chronic placental ischemia, combined with maternal systemic endothelial susceptibility and immune mal-adaptation.",
      "Site": "Placental syncytiotrophoblasts, maternal cerebral vasculature / blood-brain barrier, systemic endothelium, kidneys, and liver.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Chronic placental ischemia releases soluble anti-angiogenic factors—notably soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 (sFlt-1) and soluble endoglin (sEng)—into maternal circulation. sFlt-1 neutralizes Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor (VEGF) and Placental Growth Factor (PlGF), destroying maternal vascular endothelial integrity. In the brain, severe hypertension and loss of cerebrovascular autoregulation breach the blood-brain barrier, causing cerebral vasospasm, focal edema, and microvascular hemorrhages that excite cortical neurons, triggering generalized clonic-tonic seizures.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Malignant systemic arterial vasoconstriction, cerebral endothelial breakdown, cortical hyperexcitability and seizures, severe proteinuria, and multiorgan failure.",
      "Regulation / Feedback": "Disrupted renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system: plasma renin and aldosterone are suppressed due to renal sodium retention and high systemic pressure, but vascular sensitivity to circulating vasopressors (Angiotensin II and catecholamines) is markedly increased.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Defective spiral artery remodeling -> Chronic placental ischemia -> sFlt-1/sEng release into maternal blood -> Neutralization of VEGF -> Systemic endothelial dysfunction & vasospasm -> Severe hypertension & cerebral edema -> Generalized clonic seizures & coma.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Generalized tonic-clonic seizures**, severe headache, visual scotomas/cortical blindness, severe hypertension (BP >= 160/110 mmHg), marked generalized edema, proteinuria, and hyperreflexia.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Seizures & Headache: Severe cerebral arteriolar vasospasm and capillary hyper-permeability causing focal cerebral edema and cortical excitation.\n- Visual Disturbances: Occipital lobe ischemia and retinal arteriolar vasospasm.\n- Proteinuria: Glomerular endotheliosis (swelling of capillary endothelial cells) expanding filtration fenestrations and leaking plasma albumin.\n- Hyperreflexia: Central nervous system hyperexcitability and upper motor neuron disinhibition secondary to cerebral edema.",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Heavy proteinuria (>= 3–5 g/24h), elevated sFlt-1/PlGF ratio, elevated serum creatinine, elevated liver transaminases (ALT/AST), thrombocytopenia (platelets < 100,000/mcL), elevated serum uric acid, and elevated D-dimer / hypofibrinolysis.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "Serial blood pressure measurements, 24-hour urine protein collection or protein-to-creatinine ratio, sFlt-1/PlGF immunoassay, brain MRI (demonstrating Posterior Reversible Encephalopathy Syndrome - PRES), and fetal Doppler ultrasonography.",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "Normal BP in pregnancy = < 120/80 mmHg; Eclampsia BP = >= 160/110 mmHg. Normal 24-hr urine protein = < 150 mg; Eclampsia = >= 300 mg to > 5 g/24h. Normal platelets = 150,000–450,000/mcL; Eclampsia/HELLP = < 100,000/mcL.",
      "Compensation": "Maternal sympathetic vasoconstriction attempts to sustain organ perfusion despite severe capillary leak and intravascular hypovolemia.",
      "Decompensation": "HELLP syndrome (Hemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets), intracranial hemorrhage, pulmonary edema, acute renal failure, placental abruption, and maternal/fetal mortality.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Magnesium Sulfate (IV): First-line anticonvulsant; blocks central NMDA receptors, promotes cerebral vasodilation, and inhibits neuromuscular acetylcholine release to stop seizures.\n- Antihypertensives (Labetalol, Hydralazine, Nifedipine): Rapidly lower systemic vascular resistance to prevent maternal intracranial hemorrhage without compromising uteroplacental perfusion.\n- Definitive Cure: Immediate termination of pregnancy / delivery of the fetus and placenta to eliminate the ischemic placental source of anti-angiogenic toxins.",
      "Drugs": "Magnesium sulfate, Labetalol, Hydralazine, Nifedipine.",
      "Receptors / Channels / Transporters": "NMDA Receptors (blocked by Mg2+), L-type Calcium Channels, Alpha-1 and Beta-1 Adrenergic Receptors.",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Epilepsy, Brain tumor, Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, Meningitis/Encephalitis, Metabolic encephalopathy.",
      "Complications": "Maternal mortality, stroke/intracranial hemorrhage, placental abruption, disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), acute tubular necrosis, fetal hypoxia/demise."
