      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Complete flaccid paralysis below lesion level**, **total areflexia** (loss of deep tendon, superficial, and plantar reflexes), **total anesthesia** below the lesion, **atonic neurogenic bladder** with urinary retention, **paralytic ileus**, loss of sweating and temperature regulation below lesion, and **neurogenic shock** (profound hypotension with bradycardia in high thoracic/cervical lesions).",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Flaccid Paralysis & Areflexia: Sudden hyperpolarization of alpha and gamma motoneurons due to loss of descending reticulospinal/vestibulospinal tonic excitation.\n- Neurogenic Shock: Loss of sympathetic preganglionic drive (T1–L2) causing uninhibited parasympathetic vagal tone (bradycardia) and loss of vascular smooth muscle tone (severe vasodilation).\n- Atonic Bladder: Loss of sacral micturition reflex center facilitation leading to detrusor muscle paralysis and overflow incontinence.",
