      "Definition": "A progressive neurodegenerative extrapyramidal disorder caused by the widespread destruction of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra (pars compacta), leading to dopamine deficiency in the striatum.",
      "Normal Physiology": "- The nigrostriatal pathway projects from the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNpc) to the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen).\n- Dopamine acts on **D1 receptors** to **stimulate the Direct Pathway** (which promotes movement by disinhibiting the thalamus and motor cortex).\n- Dopamine acts on **D2 receptors** to **inhibit the Indirect Pathway** (which normally suppresses unwanted movements).\n- The balance between inhibitory dopamine and excitatory acetylcholine (ACh) in the striatum maintains normal muscle tone, posture, and smooth initiation of voluntary movement.",
      "Etiology": "Mostly idiopathic; environmental neurotoxins (e.g., MPTP / MPP+ via MAO-B action), genetic mutations (alpha-synuclein, PARK2/Parkin, LRRK2), drug-induced (antipsychotics/D2 blockers), or age-related degeneration.",
      "Site": "Substantia nigra pars compacta (SNpc) and striatum (putamen and caudate nucleus) in the basal ganglia.",
      "Pathophysiology": "1. Progressive degeneration of neuromelanin-containing dopaminergic neurons in the SNpc drops striatal dopamine by > 80%.\n2. Loss of dopamine removes D1-mediated stimulation of the direct pathway and removes D2-mediated inhibition of the indirect pathway.\n3. The indirect pathway becomes hyperactive: the subthalamic nucleus over-excites the globus pallidus internus (GPi) and substantia nigra pars reticulata.\n4. Hyperactive GPi releases massive inhibitory GABA onto the ventroanterior/ventrolateral (VA/VL) thalamus.\n5. Suppressed thalamocortical projections reduce excitatory feedback to the motor cortex, causing **akinesia** and **bradykinesia**.\n6. Loss of dopaminergic inhibition creates a relative **excess of striatal acetylcholine**, driving continuous motor loop oscillations that generate **muscle rigidity** and **resting tremor**.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Imbalance in the motor loop, hyper-inhibition of thalamocortical motor pathways, hypokinesia, and extrapyramidal hypertonia.",
      "Regulation / Feedback": "Disruption of the basal ganglia negative-feedback stabilization loops back to the premotor and primary motor cortices.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "SNpc dopaminergic neuronal death -> Striatal dopamine depletion -> Relative striatal ACh excess -> Hyperactivity of indirect pathway & subthalamic nucleus -> Excessive GPi inhibition of thalamus -> Reduced motor cortex activation -> Resting tremor, rigidity, akinesia, & postural instability.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "- **Resting Tremor:** Involuntary 3–6 Hz \"pill-rolling\" tremor present at rest and suppressed during voluntary movement.\n- **Lead-Pipe or Cogwheel Rigidity:** Uniform resistance throughout passive muscle stretch, often jerky when combined with tremor.\n- **Akinesia / Bradykinesia:** Difficulty initiating voluntary movement, extreme slowness of execution, staccato stiffness, and loss of automatic associated movements (e.g., arm swinging while walking).\n- **Festinating Gait:** Stooped posture with short, shuffling, accelerating steps as if trying to catch the center of gravity.\n- **Micrographia:** Small, cramped, illegible handwriting.\n- Mask-like facial expression, infrequent blinking, and dysphagia.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Akinesia/Bradykinesia: Excessive GABAergic inhibition of VA/VL thalamic relay nuclei by GPi, reducing motor cortex drive.\n- Rigidity: Striatal ACh over-activity outputting continuous excitatory signals to spinal motor pools.\n- Resting Tremor: Uninhibited basal ganglia-thalamic feedback loops oscillating at high gain.\n- Festinating Gait: Loss of basal ganglia postural reflexes and automatic motor pattern execution (putamen circuit).",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Histopathology reveals intracellular eosinophilic inclusions (**Lewy bodies** containing alpha-synuclein) in surviving SNpc neurons.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- SPECT / Dopamine Transporter (DaT) Imaging: Shows marked reduction in radiolabeled ligand uptake in the posterior putamen.\n- Levodopa Challenge Test: Significant improvement in motor symptoms following L-dopa administration confirms diagnosis.",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "Normal striatal DaT SPECT = robust, symmetrical comma-shaped uptake; Parkinson's disease = severe asymmetrical loss of putaminal uptake.",
      "Compensation": "Upregulation of postsynaptic striatal D1 and D2 dopamine receptors (denervation supersensitivity) in early disease stages.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Levodopa (L-Dopa) + Carbidopa: L-dopa crosses the blood-brain barrier (unlike dopamine) and is converted into dopamine by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase in surviving terminals; Carbidopa inhibits peripheral decarboxylase to prevent systemic side effects.\n- Dopamine Receptor Agonists (Bromocriptine, Pramipexole, Ropinirole): Directly stimulate postsynaptic D2/D3 receptors in the striatum.\n- MAO-B Inhibitors (Selegiline, Rasagiline): Block central degradation of dopamine and prevent MPTP toxicity.\n- Anticholinergics (Trihexyphenidyl, Benztropine): Decrease striatal ACh concentration, restoring the dopamine-to-ACh ratio.\n- Deep Brain Stimulation (DBS): High-frequency electrical stimulation of the subthalamic nucleus or GPi to suppress hyperactive inhibitory output.",
      "Drugs": "Levodopa, Carbidopa, Bromocriptine, Selegiline, Trihexyphenidyl, Entacapone (COMT inhibitor).",
      "Receptors / Channels / Transporters": "D1 and D2 Dopamine Receptors, Muscarinic ACh Receptors, Vesicular Monoamine Transporter (VMAT2), DaT.",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Essential Tremor (action/intention tremor, no rigidity), Progressive Supranuclear Palsy, Multiple System Atrophy, Drug-Induced Parkinsonism (antipsychotics), Huntington's Chorea (hyperkinetic vs hypokinetic).",
      "Complications": "Severe freezing of gait, postural instability leading to falls, L-dopa-induced dyskinesias, dysphagia leading to aspiration pneumonia, depression, and late-stage dementia."
