      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "- **Choreiform Movements:** Involuntary, rapid, jerky, flicking, \"dancing\" movements of the hands, face, trunk, and extremities.\n- **Gait Abnormality:** Unsteady, irregular, dancing gait.\n- **Dysarthria & Dysphagia:** Slurred speech and difficulty swallowing due to choreic involvement of pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles.\n- **Progressive Dementia:** Cognitive decline, memory loss, and personality changes (in Huntington's disease).\n- Milkmaid's grip (inability to sustain steady hand grip) and motor impersistence.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Choreiform Movements: Spontaneous outbursts of uninhibited VA/VL thalamic activity sending chaotic signals to the primary motor cortex.\n- Dementia: Concomitant degeneration of acetylcholine-secreting neurons in the cerebral cortex and basal forebrain.\n- Dysarthria: Involuntary choreic twitches of tongue and vocal cord motor units interrupting articulate speech.",
