      "Sequence of Changes": "Posterolateral PCA branch thrombosis -> VPL thalamic infarction -> Contralateral hemianesthesia and loss of discriminative touch -> Synaptic deafferentation & neuronal sprouting -> Delayed onset of severe central thalamic pain & hyperpathia.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Severe, extremely unpleasant central burning or lancinating pain** (thalamic hyperpathia) on the contralateral side; severe loss of discriminative touch, pressure, vibration, and position sense; **sensory ataxia and muscle weakness**; hyperalgesia and allodynia (trivial touch triggering excruciating pain). Sensation in the head and face is characteristically spared.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Central Pain / Hyperpathia: Autonomous hyperexcitability and spontaneous firing of deafferented third-order spinothalamic neurons.\n- Loss of Discriminative Touch: Destruction of third-order VPL neurons receiving medial lemniscal projections.\n- Sensory Ataxia & Weakness: Interruption of dentatorubrothalamic connections relaying cerebellar feedback to motor areas 4 and 6.\n- Sparing of Face Sensation: Posterolateral vascular lesions spare the medial thalamus and ventral posteromedial (VPM) nucleus, which processes trigeminal facial inputs.",
