      "Site": "Pretectal region / superior colliculus area of the dorsal midbrain (mesencephalon).",
      "Pathophysiology": "Neurological lesions in the pretectal region selectively destroy the light reflex relay fibers and interneurons projecting from the pretectal nuclei to the Edinger-Westphal nuclei. Loss of tonic inhibitory control over the Edinger-Westphal nuclei causes them to become chronically hyperactive, keeping the pupils tonically constricted (miotic). Because accommodation fibers descend from the visual cortex via the occipitomesencephalic tract directly to the Edinger-Westphal nucleus without relying on the pretectal relay, the accommodation-convergence reflex remains fully intact.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Disruption of the pupillary light reflex arc with complete preservation of the visual accommodation reflex pathway.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Treponema pallidum pretectal midbrain lesion -> Destruction of pretectal-Edinger-Westphal light reflex fibers -> Loss of light-induced pupillary constriction + unmasking of Edinger-Westphal chronic activity -> Small, miotic pupil unresponsive to light but responsive to accommodation (ARP: Accommodation Reflex Preserved, Pupillary Reflex Absent).",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Bilateral small, miotic pupils**; **Absence of direct and consensual pupillary light reflexes**; **Preserved pupillary constriction during accommodation / near response**; irregular pupil outline; sluggish dilation in darkness.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Absent Light Reflex: Interruption of retinal-pretectal-Edinger-Westphal light reflex afferent/interneuronal pathways.\n- Preserved Accommodation Reflex: Preservation of corticomesencephalic pathways projecting from the visual cortex directly to the Edinger-Westphal nucleus.\n- Severe Miosis: Loss of tonic inhibitory pretectal control over the Edinger-Westphal nuclei, leading to uninhibited parasympathetic sphincter pupillae tone.",
      "Laboratory Findings": "Positive serological tests for syphilis (VDRL, RPR, FTA-ABS) in blood and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF); elevated CSF protein and lymphocytic pleocytosis in active neurosyphilis.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Light-Near Dissociation Test: Shining bright light directly into the eye produces no pupillary constriction, whereas bringing a target close to the nose induces prompt pupillary constriction.\n- Slit-lamp Biomicroscopy: Evaluates pupillary border irregularity and iris atrophy.\n- CSF Analysis: Confirms tertiary neurosyphilis / tabes dorsalis.",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "Normal pupil = constricts to both light and near target; Argyll Robertson Pupil = 0% light response with 100% near/accommodation response.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Parenteral High-Dose Penicillin G (IV): Kills Treponema pallidum spirochetes to arrest neuro-inflammatory destruction in the midbrain and central nervous system.",
      "Drugs": "Aqueous Crystalline Penicillin G.",
      "Receptors / Channels / Transporters": "Muscarinic M3 Cholinergic Receptors on sphincter pupillae.",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Horner's Syndrome (unilateral miosis, ptosis, anhidrosis, intact light reflex); Adie's Tonic Pupil (dilated pupil, absent light and near responses); Marcus Gunn Pupil (relative afferent pupillary defect due to optic nerve lesion).",
      "Complications": "Permanent pupillary deformity, progression of untreated neurosyphilis to tabes dorsalis, dementia paralytica, or optic atrophy."
