      "Name with Synonyms": "Myopia, Nearsightedness, Short-Sightedness, Axial Myopia, Refractive Myopia.",
      "Definition": "A refractive error of the eye in which parallel light rays entering the eye from a distant object come to a focus in front of the retina when accommodation is completely relaxed, resulting in blurred distant vision.",
      "Normal Physiology": "In an emmetropic (normal) eye, parallel light rays coming from distant objects (more than 6 meters away) are refracted by the anterior surface of the cornea (contributing ~43 Diopters) and the crystalline lens (contributing ~17 Diopters) to come to an exact point focus on the retina without needing active ciliary muscle accommodation. Total refractive power of a normal reduced eye is approximately 59 to 60 Diopters.",
      "Etiology": "1. Axial Myopia (Most Common): Congenital or developmental elongation of the eyeball along its antero-posterior axis.\n2. Refractive / Curvature Myopia: Increased curvature of the cornea or lens, or increased refractive index of the lens (e.g., nuclear cataract).\n3. Acquired / Environmental: Prolonged, continuous close work (reading, computer screens) causing persistent ciliary muscle spasm.",
      "Site": "Optical system of the eye (cornea, lens, and elongated posterior scleral globe).",
      "Pathophysiology": "Because the antero-posterior axial length of the eyeball is too long or the refractive power of the cornea/lens is excessively strong, parallel light rays bend too sharply. The focal point falls in front of the retina in the vitreous body. By the time light rays reach the retinal photoreceptor plane, they have diverged again, forming a blurred \"blur circle\" image. No physiological mechanism exists for the eye to decrease its lens power below the relaxed state, so the eye cannot accommodate to clear distant objects. However, near objects emit diverging light rays that naturally focus further back, allowing clear vision at a specific \"far point\".",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Excessive refractive converging power relative to axial globe length, causing blurred distant vision while maintaining clear near vision.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Eyeball axial elongation or excessive lens curvature -> Parallel distant light rays focused in front of retina -> Formation of blurred image on retina -> Clear vision limited to near objects within the far point.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Blurred vision for distant objects**; **Clear vision for near objects**; **Squinting / narrowing of eyelids** when viewing distant objects (pinhole effect); headache and eyestrain (asthenopia) from chronic squinting; holding books or objects very close to the face.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Blurred Distant Vision: Distant light rays converge prematurely in front of the retina.\n- Squinting / Pinhole Effect: Narrowing the palpebral fissure cuts off peripheral diffracted light rays, increasing the depth of focus so central rays fall closer to focus on the retina.\n- Preserved Near Vision: Diverging light rays from near objects push the focal point backward onto the retina.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Snellen's Visual Acuity Chart Test: Demonstrates reduced uncorrected distant visual acuity (e.g., 20/50, 20/200) with normal near visual acuity (Jaeger chart).\n- Retinoscopy & Automated Refraction: Quantifies the negative diopter refractive error required to focus light on the retina.\n- Ocular Biometry (A-scan Ultrasound): Measures increased antero-posterior axial length (> 24 mm).",
