      "Compensation": "Moving closer to objects or squinting eyelids to reduce pupillary aperture and increase depth of focus.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Concave (Biconcave / Negative) Spherical Lenses: Diverges incoming parallel light rays before they strike the cornea, pushing the focal point backward directly onto the retina.\n- Refractive Corneal Surgery (LASIK / PRK): Flattens the central corneal curvature using an excimer laser to decrease corneal refractive power.",
      "Equations / Relationships": "- Refractive Power ( P ) in Diopters = 1 / Focal Length ( f ) in meters.\n- Lens Correction: Corrected Power = Eye Power - Concave Lens Power.",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Hypermetropia (focal point behind retina); Astigmatism (unequal focal planes); Presbyopia (age-related loss of accommodation for near objects).",
      "Complications": "High/pathological myopia increases risk of retinal detachment, open-angle glaucoma, myopic maculopathy, and premature cataract formation."
