      "Functional Abnormality": "Deficient refractive converging power relative to axial globe length, leading to chronic accommodative strain and near vision failure.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Shortened axial globe length -> Parallel light rays focused behind the retina -> Continuous compensatory ciliary muscle contraction (accommodation) -> Exhaustion of accommodative reserve -> Asthenopia, eye strain, and blurred near vision.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Asthenopia** (eyestrain, frontal headaches, burning sensation in eyes after close work); **Blurred near vision** (difficulty reading small print); Recession of the near point of vision farther away than normal; Accommodative esotropia (inward convergent squint in children).",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Asthenopia & Headaches: Chronic, continuous spasm and fatigue of the ciliary muscle trying to maintain lens accommodation.\n- Recession of Near Point: Accommodative power is exhausted accommodating for distance, leaving insufficient remaining accommodative reserve for close objects.\n- Accommodative Esotropia: High accommodative effort stimulates excessive parasympathetic oculomotor output, driving excessive medial rectus contraction and inward convergence of the visual axes.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Retinoscopy with Cycloplegia (Atropine/Cyclopentolate drops): Paralysis of ciliary muscle accommodation unmasks the full degree of latent hypermetropia.\n- Snellen & Jaeger Vision Charts: Demonstrates normal or near-normal distant visual acuity alongside degraded near visual acuity.\n- Ocular Biometry: Measures shortened antero-posterior axial length (< 22 mm).",
