      "Definition": "A physiological phenomenon occurring with advancing age (typically around 40–50 years) characterized by the progressive loss of lens elasticity and accommodation, causing the near point of vision to recede beyond normal reading or working distance.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Accommodation is driven by parasympathetic impulses via the third cranial nerve (CN III) that contract the ciliary muscle fibers. Ciliary muscle contraction pulls the ciliary body forward and medially, relaxing the tension on the suspensory zonular ligaments. The elastic lens capsule then recoils into a more spherical shape, increasing its convex curvature and refractive power (from 20 D up to ~34 D in a child) to focus near objects.",
