      "Name with Synonyms": "Glaucoma, High Intraocular Pressure Neuropathy.",
      "Definition": "An ocular disease characterized by pathologically elevated intraocular pressure (IOP) resulting from increased resistance to aqueous humor outflow, causing mechanical and ischemic compression of optic nerve axons at the optic disc, leading to nerve fiber death and blindness.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Aqueous humor is continuously secreted by ciliary process epithelium into the posterior chamber at a rate of 2 to 3 /min. It flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber, percolates through the trabecular meshwork at the iridocorneal angle, enters the canal of Schlemm, and drains into extraocular veins. Normal IOP is maintained between 10 and 20 mmHg (average ~15 mmHg) by equal rates of fluid formation and outflow.",
      "Etiology": "- Open-Angle Glaucoma: Chronic fibrous occlusion, sclerosis, or reduced permeability of the trabecular meshwork spaces.\n- Closed-Angle Glaucoma: Anterior displacement of the iris obstructing access to the trabecular meshwork at the iridocorneal angle.\n- Secondary Glaucoma: Inflammation, white blood cells, or tissue debris clogging trabecular spaces.",
      "Site": "Iridocorneal angle (trabecular meshwork / canal of Schlemm) and optic nerve head (optic disc).",
      "Pathophysiology": "Increased resistance to aqueous humor drainage through the 2–3 opening trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm causes fluid accumulation, raising IOP. High intraocular pressure (> 25–30 mmHg) mechanically compresses the optic nerve axons as they exit the eyeball through the optic disc. Compression blocks the axoplasmic flow of cytoplasm from retinal ganglion cell bodies into optic nerve fibers, depriving them of essential nutrition. Simultaneously, compression of the central retinal artery at the optic disc reduces nutrient delivery to the retina, resulting in progressive retinal ganglion cell death and optic nerve atrophy.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Impaired aqueous humor outflow, block of axoplasmic transport in optic nerve fibers, and progressive peripheral visual field loss.",
      "Regulation / Feedback": "In a normal eye, rising IOP increases the passive filtration rate of fluid into the canal of Schlemm to stabilize pressure within +/- 2 mmHg. Trabecular meshwork occlusion disrupts this self-regulatory feedback loop.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Trabecular meshwork occlusion or angle closure -> Decreased aqueous humor outflow -> Elevation of IOP (> 25–30 mmHg up to 60–70 mmHg) -> Compression of optic nerve fibers & retinal blood vessels at the optic disc -> Block of axoplasmic cytoplasmic flow -> Death of retinal ganglion cell axons -> Irreversible visual field loss & blindness.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Progressive loss of vision**, severe eye pain and rapid vision loss in acute angle closure (pressures up to 60–70 mmHg), optic disc cupping/atrophy, and peripheral field scotomas.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Vision Loss: Progressive apoptosis and death of compressed retinal ganglion cell axons forming the optic nerve.\n- Acute Severe Pain: Rapid distension of eyeball structures caused by sudden pressure spikes up to 60–70 mmHg.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Tonometry: Measures intraocular pressure by recording corneal displacement under an applied plunger force.\n- Perimetry (Bjerrum Screen): Maps central and peripheral visual field defects (e.g., arcuate scotomas).",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "- Normal IOP = 10–20 mmHg (average ~15 mmHg).\n- Glaucoma IOP = Pathologically elevated above 25–30 mmHg, rising acutely to 60–70 mmHg in severe cases.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Topical Pharmacotherapy: Drops that decrease ciliary body aqueous humor secretion or enhance trabecular/uveoscleral outflow.\n- Surgical Trabeculectomy / Laser Therapy: Operative creation of artificial channels to drain aqueous humor directly from the anterior chamber into the subconjunctival space, bypassing the blocked trabeculae.",
      "Drugs": "Eye drops reducing aqueous secretion or increasing reabsorption (e.g., beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, prostaglandin analogs).",
      "Receptors / Channels / Transporters": "Epithelial sodium active transporters in ciliary processes.",
      "Differential Diagnosis": "Ocular Hypertension (elevated IOP without optic disc damage), Cataract, Retinal Detachment, Papilledema.",
      "Complications": "Axoplasmic transport blockade, irreversible degeneration of retinal nerve fibers, and permanent blindness."
