      "Normal Physiology": "Conjugate eye movements are coordinated by three pairs of extraocular muscles (medial/lateral recti, superior/inferior recti, superior/inferior obliques) innervated by CN III, IV, and VI. Alignment allows light rays from an object to fall on **corresponding points** on both retinas (such as both foveas), which the visual cortex fuses into a single 3D image (**binocular vision and stereopsis**).",
      "Etiology": "- Non-paralytic (Concomitant): Abnormal \"set\" of the central fusion mechanism in early childhood, or uncorrected accommodative high hypermetropia.\n- Paralytic (Incomitant): Nerve palsy or trauma affecting CN III, IV, or VI, or direct extraocular muscle damage (e.g., Myasthenia Gravis).",
      "Site": "Extraocular muscles, motor nuclei/cranial nerves (CN III, IV, VI), medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF), or cortical/brainstem ocular fixation centers (areas 18, 19, frontal gaze fields).",
      "Pathophysiology": "- In paralytic strabismus, weakness of one extraocular muscle (e.g., lateral rectus in CN VI palsy) causes unopposed traction by its healthy antagonist (medial rectus), pulling the eyeball out of alignment.\n- In non-paralytic strabismus, abnormal neural tracking pathways fail to keep both visual axes aligned.\n  When visual axes misalign, light rays fall on non-corresponding retinal points.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Loss of conjugate eye alignment, failure of binocular retinal image fusion, and loss of stereopsis.",
