      "Name with Synonyms": "Conductive Deafness, Conduction Deafness, Middle Ear Air Conduction Deafness.",
      "Definition": "A type of hearing loss caused by impairment in the physical transmission or amplification of sound waves through the external or middle ear to the cochlea.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Normally, sound waves travel through the external auditory canal, vibrating the tympanic membrane. The tympanic membrane and ossicular chain (malleus, incus, stapes) amplify sound pressure (impedance matching) to transfer acoustic energy efficiently from air into the fluid-filled inner ear.",
      "Etiology": "Blockade of the external auditory canal (wax impaction, foreign body, atresia); thickening or perforation of the tympanic membrane from otitis media or trauma; Eustachian tube obstruction; ossicular immobility or destruction due to adhesions or otosclerosis (bony ankylosis of stapes footplate to the oval window).",
      "Site": "External auditory canal, tympanic membrane, Eustachian tube, or middle ear ossicular chain.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Disruption of the physical conductive mechanism prevents sound energy from reaching the oval window via air conduction. However, because the inner ear and auditory nerve remain structurally intact, sound waves conducted directly through the skull bones (bone conduction) bypass the middle ear and vibrate the basilar membrane, maintaining partial hearing capability.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Depressed air conduction across all sound frequencies (more pronounced at lower frequencies in middle ear sclerosis) with intact bone conduction.",
      "Regulation / Feedback": "Environmental noise normally transmitted through the ossicular chain masks bone-conducted sounds; in conductive deafness, this masking effect is reduced in the diseased ear.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Physical obstruction/ankylosis in external/middle ear -> Attenuation of air-conducted sound waves arriving at oval window -> Air-bone conduction gap -> Selective loss of air-conducted hearing.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Partial hearing loss where bone-conducted sounds are perceived better than air-conducted sounds; sound heard louder in the affected ear in noisy environments or during Weber's test.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Better Bone Conduction: Direct mechanical vibration of skull bones directly stimulates the basilar membrane.\n- Lateralization to Diseased Ear on Weber's Test: Environmental background noise is blocked from entering the affected ear by the conductive barrier, preventing environmental sound masking.",
