      "Pathophysiology": "Distension and elevation of hydraulic pressure within the membranous labyrinth (hydrops) causes micro-ruptures or distortion of the membranes. Excessive pressure alters the endocochlear potential and mechanical displacement of sensory hair cells in both the organ of Corti and the vestibular cristae/maculae. This intermittent displacement triggers chaotic, asymmetrical afferent discharges along the vestibulochochlear nerve.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Episodic peripheral vestibular dysfunction accompanied by fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Endolymph accumulation -> Endolymphatic hydrops -> Mechanical distension of membranous labyrinth -> Distortion of cochlear & vestibular hair cells -> Paroxysmal bursts of asymmetric nerve signals -> Attacks of vertigo + fluctuating hearing loss + tinnitus.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Classic Triad:** Sudden episodic attacks of rotational **vertigo** (duration minutes to hours); **fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss**; low-pitched **tinnitus** (roaring/ringing) and a sensation of fullness in the affected ear.",
