      "Pathophysiology": "Overstimulation of vestibular hair cells sends excessive repetitive impulses via the 8th nerve into the brainstem vestibular nuclei. From the vestibular nuclei, signals pass through the flocculonodular lobe of the cerebellum to the **chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ)** located in the area postrema on the lateral walls of the 4th ventricle. The CTZ then activates the medullary **vomiting center** in the reticular formation, triggering autonomic discharge and motor patterns of vomiting. Conflict between visual inputs, proprioception, and vestibular signals exacerbates CTZ stimulation.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Hyper-excitation of vestibulocerebellar-CTZ pathways leading to autonomic instability and gastrointestinal emesis.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Excessive repetitive physical motion -> Overstimulation of semicircular canal & macular hair cells -> Vestibular nerve impulse surge -> Vestibular nuclei & vestibulocerebellum excitation -> CTZ (area postrema) activation -> Medullary vomiting center stimulation -> Nausea, pallor, sweating, & vomiting.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "Prodromal **nausea**, epigastric discomfort, pale skin, cold sweating, palpitations, dizziness, and **repeated vomiting**.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Nausea: Conscious recognition of subconscious medullary excitation near the vomiting center.\n- Sweating & Palpitations: Autonomic sympathetic/parasympathetic reflex responses driven by medullary reticular activation.\n- Vomiting Act: Synchronized contraction of abdominal wall muscles, downward diaphragm contraction, closure of glottis/soft palate, and lower esophageal sphincter relaxation.",
      "Compensation": "- Closing Eyes: Eliminates conflicting visual motion signals entering the CTZ.\n- Habitual Exposure: Central nervous system synaptic adaptation suppresses feedback loops over time.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Antiemetics / Antihistamines / Anticholinergics: Block H1 and muscarinic receptors in the CTZ and vestibular pathways to prevent activation of the vomiting center.\n- Intractable Surgical Option: Ablation of the vestibulocerebellum (flocculonodular lobe) or labyrinthectomy eliminates the neural pathway and cures motion sickness.",
      "Receptors / Channels / Transporters": "Histamine H1 Receptors, Muscarinic M1 ACh Receptors, Neurokinin NK1 Receptors.",
      "Exceptions": "Destruction of the CTZ in the area postrema or loss of vestibular inner ear function renders an individual completely immune to motion sickness."
