      "Name with Synonyms": "Vertigo, Illusion of Motion, Rotational Dizziness.",
      "Definition": "An abnormal sensory illusion of motion—typically a sensation of spinning or rotation of the self or the environment—when no actual movement is occurring.",
      "Normal Physiology": "Spatial orientation is maintained by congruent inputs from the vestibular system (semicircular canals for rotation; utricle/saccule for linear position), visual system, and somatosensory proprioceptors integrated in the vestibular nuclei, cerebellum, and cerebral cortex.",
      "Etiology": "1. Physiological Vertigo: Motion sickness or post-rotational motion.\n2. Peripheral / Labyrinthine Vertigo: Ménière's disease, Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV), labyrinthitis, vestibular neuronitis.\n3. Central Vertigo: Lesions of the 8th cranial nerve, vertebrobasilar arterial insufficiency, brainstem stroke, or MS plaques.",
      "Site": "Vestibular apparatus (semicircular canals/maculae), vestibulocochlear nerve, brainstem vestibular nuclei, or vestibulocerebellum.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Asymmetrical resting neural activity between the left and right vestibular systems creates an imbalance in tonic impulse traffic entering the vestibular nuclei. The brainstem and cortex misinterpret this neural asymmetry as continuous physical head rotation, driving abnormal vestibulo-ocular reflexes (nystagmus) and vestibulospinal postural instability.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Sensory-motor mismatch, loss of spatial orientation, abnormal vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) gain, and motor ataxia.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Asymmetrical vestibular hair cell firing or nerve lesion -> Imbalance in baseline input to brainstem vestibular nuclei -> Misinterpretation by cortex as body rotation -> Illusion of spinning (Vertigo) + involuntary eye oscillations (Nystagmus) + postural sway.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Illusion of rotational spinning**, **nystagmus** (rhythmic, involuntary oscillation of the eyes), postural instability/staggering, nausea, and sweating.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Rotational Illusion: Imbalance in firing rates between left and right vestibular nuclei.\n- Nystagmus: The **slow component** is driven by asymmetrical vestibular signals via the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) pushing the eyes to one side; the **rapid component** is a corrective central saccade integrated in the superior colliculus and prepontine reticular formation.",
      "Diagnostic / Physiological Tests": "- Romberg's Test: Tests postural balance with eyes open vs. closed (positive in proprioceptive/vestibular loss).\n- Rotational Stimulation (Bàrány Chair Test): Subject seated with head tilted 30° forward is spun 10 revolutions over 20 seconds and stopped; evaluates post-rotational nystagmus (normal duration 20–30s).\n- Caloric Stimulation Test: Ear canal is irrigated with cold (30°C) or warm (44°C) water. Warm water creates convection currents in endolymph that depolarize horizontal semicircular canal hair cells, inducing nystagmus toward the irrigated side for ~90 seconds.\n- Electronystagmography: Electrophysiological recording of eye movements.",
      "Normal vs. Abnormal Values": "- Caloric Test: Absence or marked reduction of caloric nystagmus indicates canal paresis / loss of vestibular labyrinth function on that side.",
      "Treatment + Physiological Basis": "- Vestibular Suppressants (Meclizine, Diazepam): Depress central vestibular nuclear excitability.\n- Epley Maneuver: Repositions canaliths in BPPV."
