      "Definition": "Complete loss of the sense of smell. (Partial loss is termed hyposmia; distorted perception is dysosmia or parosmia).",
      "Normal Physiology": "Volatile, water- and lipid-soluble odorant molecules enter the nasal cavity and bind to GPCR odorant receptors on unmyelinated cilia of bipolar olfactory receptor cells in the olfactory mucosa. Activation of Golf G-protein stimulates adenylyl cyclase, generating cAMP, which opens gated sodium/calcium channels to depolarize the cell (-55 mV to -30 mV). Axons pass through the cribriform plate to synapse in glomeruli of the olfactory bulb onto mitral and tufted cells, which project via lateral and medial striae to the piriform cortex, amygdala, entorhinal cortex, and orbitofrontal cortex without relaying in the thalamus.",
      "Etiology": "Common cold / rhinitis (most common cause); head injury causing fracture of the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone; injury to olfactory nerves, bulb, or tract; aging; Kallmann's syndrome (anosmia associated with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism); orbitofrontal cortex lesions.",
      "Site": "Olfactory epithelium/cilia, cribriform plate, olfactory bulb/tract, or orbitofrontal/piriform olfactory cortex.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Physical obstruction prevents odorants from reaching the olfactory mucosa, or shearing force/trauma severs olfactory nerve rootlets passing through the narrow perforations of the cribriform plate. Alternatively, destruction of bipolar receptor cells or cortical lesions in the orbitofrontal cortex abolishes olfactory signal generation or conscious discrimination.",
