      "Normal Physiology": "Volatile or water- and lipid-soluble odorants dissolve in the thin mucus layer covering the olfactory epithelium in the upper nasal cavity. Odorants bind to specific G-protein coupled receptors (coupled to G_olf ) located on the cilia of bipolar olfactory receptor neurons. Activation of G_olf stimulates adenylyl cyclase to produce cyclic AMP (cAMP), which opens cyclic nucleotide-gated (CNG) cation channels, allowing Ca2+ and Na+ influx. This depolarizes the receptor neuron from -55 mV to -30 mV or less, generating action potentials. Unmyelinated axons pass through perforations in the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to enter the olfactory bulb. Within globular structures called **olfactory glomeruli**, primary afferents synapse with dendrites of second-order neurons (**mitral cells** and **tufted cells**). The axons of these cells form the olfactory tract, projecting via lateral and medial striae directly to primary and association olfactory cortices (piriform cortex, amygdala, entorhinal cortex, and orbitofrontal cortex) without a mandatory thalamic relay.",
