      "Functional Abnormality": "Elevation of the olfactory perception threshold, leading to impaired odor detection and secondary reduction in flavor perception.",
      "Regulation / Feedback": "Granule cells and periglomerular cells in the olfactory bulb provide lateral and feedback inhibition (releasing GABA) onto mitral and tufted cells to sharpen signal transmission. CNG channel inhibition by the Ca2+--calmodulin complex normally drives rapid adaptation.",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Etiological factor (e.g., viral rhinitis or mucosal atrophy) -> Reduction in functional olfactory receptors or mechanical blockage -> Elevated stimulation threshold -> Fewer action potentials generated along CN I -> Reduced signal transmission to piriform/orbitofrontal cortex -> Hyposmia.",
      "Clinical Features / Signature Symptoms": "**Reduced sensitivity to odors**, difficulty detecting subtle smells (e.g., food, perfume, smoke), and perceived loss of food flavor.",
      "Physiological Basis of Features": "- Reduced Olfactory Sensitivity: Elevated concentration threshold needed to trigger sufficient depolarizing action potentials in primary olfactory neurons.\n- Impaired Food Flavor: Loss of retronasal olfactory input to the orbitofrontal cortex during eating, where taste and smell are integrated to perceive flavor.",
