      "Definition": "Total loss or absence of the sense of taste (gustation).",
      "Normal Physiology": "Taste buds are located in circumvallate, fungiform, and foliate papillae of the tongue, as well as the mucosa of the palate, pharynx, epiglottis, and esophagus. Tastants dissolve in saliva and enter taste pores to interact with microvilli of gustatory receptor cells (modified epithelial cells with a 10-day lifespan). Receptor cell depolarization opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, triggering exocytosis of glutamate onto primary sensory axons:\n- Anterior 2/3 of tongue: Innervated by chorda tympani (branch of CN VII / Facial nerve).\n- Posterior 1/3 of tongue: Innervated by Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX).\n- Epiglottis & Pharynx: Innervated by Vagus nerve (CN X).\n  First-order neurons terminate in the **nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)** in the medulla. Second-order neurons ascend in the ipsilateral medial lemniscus to the **ventral posteromedial (VPM) nucleus of the thalamus**. Third-order neurons project to the **primary gustatory cortex** (footplate of the postcentral gyrus and insular-opercular area).",
      "Etiology": "- Xerostomia (dry mouth): Lack of salivary secretion (e.g., Sjögren's syndrome, cystic fibrosis) preventing tastants from dissolving to reach receptors.\n- Nutritional Deficiencies: Zinc deficiency or Vitamin B3 (niacin) deficiency.\n- Medications: Drugs containing sulfhydryl groups (captopril, penicillamine), cisplatin, or prolonged tobacco use.\n- Receptor/Cellular Damage: Herpes virus infection, radiation therapy, or aging.\n- Neurological Lesions: Injury to CN VII, CN IX, CN X, NTS, VPM thalamus, or primary gustatory cortex due to trauma, neoplasm, stroke, diabetes mellitus, or hypothyroidism.",
      "Site": "Tongue papillae/taste buds, salivary glands, cranial nerves VII/IX/X, nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS), VPM thalamic nucleus, or primary gustatory cortex.",
      "Pathophysiology": "Lack of saliva prevents tastants from dissolving and reaching microvilli in taste pores, or structural destruction of taste cells/cranial nerve pathways interrupts the generation or central transmission of gustatory action potentials. Because all incoming gustatory signals are blocked from reaching the VPM thalamus and gustatory cortex, taste perception is completely lost.",
      "Functional Abnormality": "Total loss of chemosensory gustatory signal generation and transmission across all primary modalities (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami).",
      "Sequence of Changes": "Etiological insult (e.g., Bell's palsy, Sjögren's syndrome, zinc deficiency) -> Interruption of tastant solution / destruction of taste buds / nerve transection -> Abolition of receptor potential generation -> Absence of action potentials along CN VII/IX/X -> Loss of signal relay to NTS, VPM, and gustatory cortex -> Ageusia.",
